        "a": "Oral thrush in an HIV-positive patient is a meaningful clinical signal. It is classified as an AIDS-defining opportunistic infection when CD4 count falls below approximately 200 cells/mm³, though it can occur at higher counts. If you are currently on antiretroviral therapy (ART) and develop oral thrush, it may suggest suboptimal viral suppression or a recent immune dip. TDMD will treat the acute thrush episode, but we will also recommend urgent follow-up with your HIV care provider for CD4 count and viral load assessment. Recurrent oral candidiasis in an HIV-positive patient on ART may warrant prophylaxis with fluconazole 100–200mg three times weekly. This is a clinical situation where coordination with your HIV specialist is as important as the acute antifungal treatment."
